o-apex with brake applied;\n coast = frames near apex with neither gas nor brake; peak_brake_g = peak decel G)\n\nGRIP UTILIZATION ANALYSIS (friction circle):\n(peak_total_g = grip envelope; grip_fill% = avg/peak = how much grip is used on average;\n combined_brake% = % of braking frames with lat_g > 0.3 = trail braking effectiveness)\n\n  Session peak combined G: 0.00G\n\nTHEORETICAL BEST LAP:\n  Theoretical best: 99.00s (actual best: 100.00s)\n  Potential gain: 1.00s across 2 corners\n  Per-corner best segments vs best lap segments:\n    C1 T1: best segment 2.50s  |  best lap segment 3.00s  |  gap +0.50s\n    C2 T2: best segment 3.50s  |  best lap segment 4.00s  |  gap +0.50s\n\nTIME LOSS RANKING (worst -> best, by segment time delta):\n  (Deltas capped at ±10s; LOW-confidence deltas > ±3s flagged as suspect.)\n  T2                             -0.50s\n  T1                             -0.50s\n\n============================================================\nRESPONSE FORMAT — FOLLOW EXACTLY. NO DEVIATION.\n\nCRITICAL STYLE RULE: Be extremely concise. Each bullet is ONE short actionable sentence.\nThe driver reads this at a glance between sessions. Information overload = useless.\nGood example: 'Brake 1s sooner and turn in later to setup for exit (+0.7s).'\nBad example: 'Lap 4 applies 1.02G peak braking with 25% trail brake into a corner where Lap 2 peaks at 0.35G — remove the brake input entirely.'\nOne supporting number per bullet maximum. No multi-stat comparisons. No lap-vs-lap narration.\n\nOutput in clean Markdown. Use ## for section headers.\nUse bullet points inside sections. Never use numbered lists inside bullets.\nNo padding. No preamble.\nStart your response directly with '## [Car] — [Track Name] Debrief'\n\n---\n\n## 1. TOP 3 TIME-LOSS CORNERS\n\nExclude any corners flagged as corrupt/capped in the data above.\nFormat each corner EXACTLY like this:\n\n- **[Corner Name]** | -[delta]s | [short action to fix it]\n\nThe action must be a direct instruction (e.g. 'lift instead of braking', 'brake 0.5s earlier', 'carry 15 km/h more apex speed').\nDo NOT explain the cause in detail — just state what to change.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DRIVING TECHNIQUE\n\n5 bullets maximum. Each bullet is ONE short instruction the driver can act on immediately.\nFormat: **[Corner]:** [do X] ([one supporting number]).\n\nExamples of correct brevity:\n  - **Turn 3:** Brake 0.5s earlier and trail deeper to hold 94 km/h apex.\n  - **Rainey Curve:** Commit to turn-in 0.3s sooner — no braking after initial lift.\n  - **Turn 10:** Get on throttle at apex — coasting loses 17 km/h exit speed.\n\nDo NOT say 'consider' or 'try'. Do NOT cite multiple numbers or compare laps inline.\n\n---\n\n## 3. CONSISTENCY\n\n3 bullets maximum. One line each.\nFormat: **[Corner]:** [apex range] km/h spread — [one-phrase cause: 'no braking marker' or 'commitment varies'].\n\n---\n\n## 4. CAR SETUP — Test Car\n\nOutput a Markdown table with exactly these columns:\n| Parameter | Signal | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n\nRules:\n- Maximum 4 rows. Only where telemetry gives a CLEAR signal.\n- 'Signal' = one data point (e.g. '28.4 psi hot', 'peak brake temp 620C').\n- 'Change' = short directional action (e.g. 'reduce 0.5 psi', 'raise rear 1 step').\n- Parameter and Signal MUST describe the same subsystem. Never mix evidence across systems.\n- Tyre pressure rows MUST use tyre pressure evidence in psi only -- never brake temperature, tyre temperature, or wear.\n- Brake temperature evidence may only support brake-related parameters, and only if that brake-related parameter is listed as adjustable for this car.\n- If you do not have a matching telemetry signal for a parameter, omit that row entirely.\n- Do NOT assume brake bias is adjustable -- many cars have fixed brake bias.\n- Only recommend parameters you are certain this car can adjust in AC Evo.\n\n---\n\n## 5. STRAIGHTS & SECTORS\n\n3 bullets maximum. One line each.\nUse the STRAIGHT/SECTOR ANALYSIS and EXIT-TO-ENTRY CORRELATION